      "agree": true,
      "kind": "agree",
      "critical": true,
      "builder_quote_located": true,
      "critic_quote_located": true
    },
    {
      "field": "study.population",
      "builder": "1,767 patients with cognitive symptoms (subjective cognitive decline, mild cognitive impairment or dementia) recruited while undergoing a memory investigation in clinical practice. CARE SETTING: 1,219 from four secondary care specialist memory clinics / outpatient neurodegenerative clinics — Memory Clinic of Skåne University Hospital (Malmö, Sweden, n=337), Memory Clinic of Sahlgrenska University Hospital (Gothenburg, Sweden, n=165), Cognitive and Behavioural Neurology Unit (Barcelona, Spain, n=487) and the outpatient neurodegenerative clinic of Brescia University Hospital (Italy, n=230); and 548 from a primary care cohort recruited from 19 primary care units in the south of Sweden, where patients sought medical help in primary care for cognitive symptoms. REFERENCE STANDARD: AD pathology defined by the CSF Aβ42:p-tau181 ratio measured with the FDA-approved Lumipulse assay (cutoff <11.94), or a positive [18F]flutemetamol PET visual read for the 87 primary care participants who could not undergo lumbar puncture.",
      "critic": "participants with cognitive symptoms",
      "agree": true,
      "kind": "agree",
      "critical": true,
      "builder_quote_located": true,
      "critic_quote_located": true
    },
    {
      "field": "study.comparator",
      "builder": "Comparator index tests: the plasma p-tau217:Aβ42 (Lumipulse) ratio and a mass-spectrometry-based method for plasma %p-tau217 (p-tau217:np-tau217 × 100), both compared against Lumipulse plasma p-tau217 as secondary analyses",
      "critic": null,
